## 1. Turn liability into a moat, not just a disclaimer

Right now "Copilot, not autonomous engine" is a legal shield. Make it a sales weapon instead:
- Get **professional indemnity insurance** before you sign customer #1 — this alone differentiates you from every scrappy AI wrapper competitor who can't back their output.
- Partner with (or hire) a **licensed customs clearing agent as co-founder or paid advisor**. Their name/license on your advisory board turns "trust me" into "a registered SAQA-accredited declarant reviewed this system."
- Build an **audit trail feature**: every AI suggestion timestamped, every human override logged. When (not if) a dispute happens, you have forensic proof the clerk made the final call. This is a product feature you can sell, not just a legal defense.

## 2. Build the data flywheel explicitly — this is your real moat

First-mover advantage in a 500-operator market erodes fast once competitors see you succeeding. What doesn't erode: **proprietary correction data**.
- Every time a clerk overrides your suggested HS code, log it. After 6 months you have a dataset of real-world misclassification patterns no competitor can replicate without your customer base.
- This is the pitch to investors later: "We're not selling software, we're building the largest verified HS-code correction dataset in Southern Africa."

## 3. Pick ONE vertical and dominate before expanding

The report says "focus on high-risk verticals first" but doesn't force the discipline. Make it explicit:
- Choose **one** (Solar & Batteries is probably best — booming market, heavy NRCS rules, and it's a growing import category so there's urgency).
- Get to 80% market share among Gauteng/KZN forwarders clearing solar/battery imports *before* touching textiles or steel.
- Depth beats breadth here — a forwarder who sees you nail every edge case in their exact commodity type becomes a referral engine into their industry peers.

## 4. De-risk the SARS scraper with a human-in-the-loop fallback

"Automated scraper" as a single point of failure is dangerous. Add:
- A **weekly manual verification pass** by a compliance person (even part-time/contracted) against your automated scrape, especially around budget speeches.
- A **client-visible "last verified" timestamp** on tariff data — turns a potential liability into a transparency feature that builds trust.

## 5. Prove the ROI number, don't just claim it

Turn the "5x ROI" pitch from theory into evidence:
- With your first 3 design partners, build a simple dashboard tracking **rand value of penalties/delays prevented** per client per month.
- Use this as your only sales collateral. A screenshot showing "R47,000 saved this month" closes deals faster than any feature list.

---

**The honest ceiling:** even doing all of this, you're at 9.8-9.9, not a clean 10 — because the remaining risk (a SARS inspector, a bad month, an edge case nobody anticipated) isn't fully within your control in a regulatory-dependent business. That's fine. 9.8 with disciplined execution beats 10 in theory.

System Rules & Principles
Frontend: TypeScript, Next.js 14+ App Router, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, TanStack Query, React Hook Form, Zod.

Backend: Python 3.12, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2.0 (typed), Pydantic v2 schemas, Alembic, Celery, Redis.

Database: PostgreSQL 16 + pgvector. Multi-tenant: EVERY DB query must filter by organization_id unless explicitly global.

Compliance & Security: Multi-tenant isolation. No plain-text passwords (Argon2/bcrypt). Secrets in env. Immutable audit logs for all mutations. Strict POPIA compliance (no public LLM training on client data).

AI Engine: Pre-clearance copilot (NOT autonomous / no direct SARS submission). Deterministic rules for regulatory checks; AI for OCR extraction & HS code classification suggestions (must include confidence scores). Human review mandatory for low confidence (<0.85) or high-risk items.

Core Data Model (SQLAlchemy 2.0 / PostgreSQL)
organizations: id (UUID), name, slug, plan

users: id, email, password_hash, full_name, is_active

memberships: id, organization_id, user_id, role (owner|admin|compliance_manager|clerk|viewer|api_service)

api_keys: id, organization_id, name, key_hash, is_active

parties: id, organization_id, party_type (importer|exporter|supplier|consignee|clearing_agent), name, vat_number, customs_code, address

shipments: id, organization_id, reference, shipment_type (import|export|transit), transport_mode (sea|air|road|rail), status (draft|extracting|needs_review|approved|packet_generated), risk_level (low|medium|high|critical), incoterms, currency, foreign keys to parties

documents: id, organization_id, shipment_id, document_type (commercial_invoice|packing_list|bill_of_lading|certificate_of_origin|import_permit|nrcs_loa|dangerous_goods_declaration|other), file_key, extraction_status

extracted_fields: id, document_id, shipment_id, field_name, field_value (JSONB), confidence, verified

line_items: id, shipment_id, document_id, description, sku, quantity, unit_price, total_price, currency, hs_code_suggested, final_hs_code, confidence, needs_review

tariff_records: id, hs_code, sa_tariff_code, description, duty_rate, vat_rate (15%), embedding (VECTOR(1536))

compliance_checks: id, organization_id, shipment_id, rule_pack (SARS_TARIFF|ITAC_PERMITS|NRCS_LOA|SADC_ORIGIN|DANGEROUS_GOODS|VALUATION_ANOMALIES), severity (info|warning|error|critical), message, recommended_action

duty_estimates: id, shipment_id, customs_value, customs_duty, vat, total_estimated, disclaimer

audit_logs: id, organization_id, user_id, action, entity_type, entity_id, before_value (JSONB), after_value (JSONB), metadata (JSONB)

Core API Routes (/api/v1)
POST /auth/register, POST /auth/login (Returns JWT)

POST /organizations, GET /organizations/me

POST /shipments, GET /shipments, GET /shipments/{id}

POST /shipments/{id}/documents (Upload file), POST /documents/{id}/process

GET /documents/{id}/extraction (Get header & line-item extractions + confidence)

POST /shipments/{id}/classify (RAG vector search + LLM ranking for Top-5 HS codes)

POST /shipments/{id}/compliance/check (Deterministic rule engine pass)

POST /shipments/{id}/estimate (Duty/VAT computation)

POST /shipments/{id}/packet (Outputs JSON, CSV, PDF summary)

GET /tariffs/search?q={query}

Background Jobs (Celery + Redis)
document_extraction: Ingests file → OCR → LLM extraction → JSON Schema validation → Populates extracted_fields & line_items.

shipment_classification: Generates embedding for line item → Vector search on tariff_records → LLM re-rank → Saves candidates with confidence.

compliance_check: Evaluates deterministic rule packs (missing invoices, ITAC permit flags, NRCS LOA requirements, SADC origin mismatches, dangerous goods checks) → Assigns shipment risk score.

packet_generation: Generates verified compliance packet (PDF/JSON/CSV) + records immutable audit log.

